{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ה' אלהיכם", :translit "Hashem Elokeichem", :gloss "YHWH your God"} {:he "וכי תזבחו", :translit "vekhi tizbachu", :gloss "and when you sacrifice"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah parshanut: the word 'sacrifice' in the adjacent verse (Lev 19:5) is read back onto the prohibition of idols in v. 4, since sacrifice to an idol violates 'do not turn to idols.'"}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 19:4 prohibits turning to idols and calls God 'YHWH your Elohim'; Leviticus 19:5 immediately follows with 'and when you sacrifice a peace-offering.' Ba'al HaTurim links the two: since the sacrificial animal must be slaughtered in God's Name, offering it to any other name is an expression of idolatry. The requirement that slaughter be consecrated to God's Name is established at Leviticus 22:27–28, where an ox or sheep brought as an offering must be 'before Hashem,' and the verse explicitly forbids slaughtering an animal 'in the field' (Lev 17:3–4), meaning outside the consecrated framework of God's Name.", :sources ["Leviticus 19:4" "Leviticus 19:5" "Leviticus 22:27-28" "Leviticus 17:3-4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah parshanut: the word 'sacrifice' in the adjacent verse (Lev 19:5) is read back onto the prohibition of idols in v. 4, since sacrifice to an idol violates 'do not turn to idols.'", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:07.509439868Z", :model "glm-5.2"}